e9ed8b3f1b3c"],

Cell["\<\
This notebook gives and overview of Fisher information matrices and principal \
component analysis in the context of gravitational wave astronomy. 

A Fisher information matrix (FIM) is a way to approximate the shape of a \
probability distribution as a Gaussian distribution. We use it to make \
forecasts about  about what kinds of measurements we will be able to make \
with different models and detectors. 

See arXiv:gr-qc/0703086  or arXiv:1502.05735 for an overviews. 
See arXiv:2510.11861 for an example of using a Fisher analysis to make \
predictions about what we can learn from future detectors.  
See arXiv:1808.05286 for an example of using a Fisher analysis to make \
predictions about the constraints we will be able to place on dark matter.  \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.987520785591744*^9, 3.987520859173603*^9}, {
  3.987520986129735*^9, 3.987520989241692*^9}, {3.987528369912759*^9, 
  3.987528394651223*^9}, {3.987528424761643*^9, 3.987528637712328*^9}, {
  3.9875286836626253`*^9, 3.987528691395687*^9}, {3.9875289005577*^9, 
  3.987528905779846*^9}, {3.9875289857871017`*^9, 
  3.987528996120275*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3a80c268-b498-40a0-80db-\
f3ee42f595bb"],

